---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import TokenTable from "../../components/docs/TokenTable.astro";
import { codeTheme } from "../../components/code-theme";
import { darkModeExample, overrideRedExample } from "../../data/snippets";
import { noFlashScript } from "../../scripts/theme";

const code = "mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Theming | Buril UI"
  description="Buril's design tokens, dark mode and how to change the palette."
>
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl">Theming</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
    Components read the standard shadcn tokens, so any shadcn theme works. The optional Buril theme
    sets them to ink on paper, with one red for focus, decoration, the hover wash and destructive
    actions.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Token layers</h2>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <strong>Primitives</strong>, <code>--buril-*</code>: the raw palette, such as
      <code>--buril-paper</code> and <code>--buril-ink</code>. They never change with the color
      scheme. Only this docs site uses the palette; the registry theme writes the final color
      values, plus the 10 motion tokens, <code>--buril-duration-*</code> and
      <code>--buril-ease-*</code>, which components read with a fallback.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>shadcn variables</strong>, such as <code>--background</code> and
      <code>--primary</code>. They switch between light and dark, and they are the only tokens
      components depend on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Buril extras</strong>: <code>--decor</code> for decoration and <code>--grid</code> for
      grid lines. Decoration always has a fallback, so components still work without them.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="mt-4">
    An <code>@theme inline</code> block maps each variable to a Tailwind color, so
    <code>bg-background</code> and <code>text-primary</code> follow the active scheme. The theme
    also defines the font tokens and a <code>@custom-variant dark</code> rule, explained below. It
    leaves <code>--sidebar-*</code> and <code>--chart-*</code> alone, because Buril has no sidebar
    or chart components.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Tokens</h2>
  <TokenTable />

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Dark mode</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The theme reads two classes on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>: <code>.dark</code> turns dark mode on
    and <code>.light</code> forces light mode. With neither class, a
    <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> media query follows the system, so the page matches the user's
    setting without JavaScript.
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={darkModeExample} lang="html" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The theme redefines Tailwind's <code>dark:</code> variant with the same rule, so
    <code>dark:</code> utilities agree with the tokens. A toggle must therefore set
    <code>.light</code> to force light mode; removing <code>.dark</code> only returns to the system
    setting. With next-themes, <code>attribute="class"</code> already does this.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Avoid a flash</h3>
  <p class="mt-4">
    If you store the visitor's choice, apply it before the first paint with an inline script at the
    top of <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. This site runs this one:
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={noFlashScript} lang="js" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Override the red</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The red carries meaning, so it is limited to a few tokens: <code>--decor</code> for decoration,
    <code>--ring</code> for the focus ring, <code>--accent</code> for the hover wash behind outline
    and ghost buttons, and <code>--destructive</code> for destructive actions and errors. To brand
    with your own color, redefine <code>--decor</code>, <code>--ring</code>, <code>--accent</code>
    and <code>--accent-foreground</code> after the theme, in all three blocks it writes, and keep
    <code>--destructive</code> red:
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={overrideRedExample} lang="css" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      Keep <code>--ring</code> at least 3:1 against <code>--background</code>, so focus stays
      visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep <code>--destructive</code> at least 4.5:1 against <code>--background</code>, because
      error text uses it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep <code>--accent-foreground</code> at least 4.5:1 against <code>--accent</code>, and
      <code>--ring</code> at least 3:1 against <code>--accent</code>, because hovered buttons show
      text and focus on it.
    </li>
    <li>Measure both schemes: a color that passes on paper can fail on the dark background.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Next steps</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/guides/dark-mode" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Dark mode
      </a>{" "}
      adds a theme picker with next-themes, in Astro or in Vite.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/guides/theme" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Customizing the theme
      </a>
      shows a neutral palette, your own fonts and calmer motion.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
